WEBVTT

0
00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:06.460
Welcome to using AI to support learning.

1
00:00:06.659 --> 00:00:09.939
Generative AI is becoming part of everyday academic life,

2
00:00:09.949 --> 00:00:11.890
and when used thoughtfully and ethically,

3
00:00:12.149 --> 00:00:13.970
it can be a valuable study partner.

4
00:00:14.939 --> 00:00:17.450
This module explores the essential question,

5
00:00:17.649 --> 00:00:20.489
how can AI tools genuinely support student learning

6
00:00:20.489 --> 00:00:23.520
and academic growth without replacing your own thinking,

7
00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:24.440
research,

8
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.110
or academic work?

9
00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.129
By the end of this module,

10
00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:31.870
you should be able to explain how AI can support learning.

11
00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:36.159
Identify ethical ways AI can assist with brainstorming,

12
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.759
explaining concepts,

13
00:00:37.849 --> 00:00:39.790
receiving feedback on assessments,

14
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.580
and general study support.

15
00:00:42.439 --> 00:00:46.200
Use prompts appropriately to get useful support from AI tools.

16
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.319
Brainstorming.

17
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.400
Brainstorming is the process of generating ideas

18
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.659
and organising your thinking about a topic.

19
00:00:53.509 --> 00:00:55.939
AI can help when you are unsure where to begin.

20
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:57.909
For example,

21
00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:00.509
you can ask it to suggest different angles,

22
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.430
arguments,

23
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.439
or structures for an assignment.

24
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.489
The important step is to choose an option that genuinely reflects your own view,

25
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.660
and then develop it using your own research and reasoning.

26
00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:15.190
You could also ask AI to create a rough outline

27
00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.589
of headings or a logical flow for your writing.

28
00:01:18.629 --> 00:01:20.900
Use this only as a starting point,

29
00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:22.809
then build the content yourself.

30
00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.930
Another useful approach is to ask AI to act as a devil's advocate.

31
00:01:28.639 --> 00:01:31.190
It can offer counterarguments to your position,

32
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.099
helping you test and strengthen your reasoning.

33
00:01:34.639 --> 00:01:35.589
For example,

34
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:36.540
you could ask,

35
00:01:36.879 --> 00:01:38.839
I'm writing an assignment arguing the

36
00:01:38.839 --> 00:01:41.000
strengths of cognitive behavioural therapy.

37
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.690
Suggest 3 different ways I could open this argument,

38
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.489
each with a different rhetorical approach.

39
00:01:48.279 --> 00:01:51.029
Explain the strengths and limitations of each.

40
00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:54.440
I will choose which one to develop myself.

41
00:01:55.379 --> 00:01:56.900
Explaining concepts.

42
00:01:57.139 --> 00:02:01.959
AI can also help when a textbook explanation or lecture has not yet made sense.

43
00:02:02.639 --> 00:02:06.099
Ask it to explain a challenging concept in simpler language,

44
00:02:06.379 --> 00:02:07.489
use an analogy,

45
00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:09.960
or provide a relevant real world example.

46
00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:14.570
Keep the conversation active by asking follow-up questions.

47
00:02:15.389 --> 00:02:16.229
For instance,

48
00:02:16.429 --> 00:02:21.490
why does this matter in practise or how is this different from a related concept?

49
00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.589
These questions move you beyond memorising

50
00:02:24.589 --> 00:02:27.289
definitions and towards genuine understanding.

51
00:02:28.059 --> 00:02:31.839
AI can also provide an initial summary of a dense academic text.

52
00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:33.619
However,

53
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.100
always return to the original source.

54
00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.520
AI summaries may miss important details,

55
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:40.809
remove nuance,

56
00:02:41.059 --> 00:02:42.839
or contain inaccuracies.

57
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.919
Try a prompt such as

58
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.199
explain Socratic questioning as if I'm a first-year student,

59
00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.759
using one real world example.

60
00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.220
Then ask me a question to check my understanding.

61
00:02:55.259 --> 00:02:57.029
Feedback on assessments.

62
00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:00.809
AI can give useful feedback on a draught before you submit it.

63
00:03:01.619 --> 00:03:03.720
You can ask it to review clarity,

64
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:04.639
structure

65
00:03:04.899 --> 00:03:05.570
and flow,

66
00:03:05.779 --> 00:03:08.639
or to identify a weakness or gap in your argument.

67
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.059
Editorial tools,

68
00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.000
such as grammarly or Trinker,

69
00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:15.440
can also help you identify grammar,

70
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.630
punctuation,

71
00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:19.059
and academic tone issues in your writing.

72
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.000
You can use AI to check the completeness of your work,

73
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.559
including whether the citations appear consistently formatted.

74
00:03:27.399 --> 00:03:28.080
However,

75
00:03:28.289 --> 00:03:31.759
you remain responsible for verifying that every source is real,

76
00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:32.710
credible

77
00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:34.509
and accurately represented.

78
00:03:35.259 --> 00:03:35.860
Remember,

79
00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:37.800
AI feedback should complement,

80
00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.139
not replace feedback from lecturers,

81
00:03:40.339 --> 00:03:41.020
peers,

82
00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:43.520
and academic support services.

83
00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.500
AI cannot assess your work against your actual course rubric or

84
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.300
subject specific expectations in the way a qualified educator can.

85
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:52.610
Study support.

86
00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.639
AI can support active study.

87
00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.770
Instead of passively rereading your notes,

88
00:03:58.089 --> 00:04:01.750
ask it to quiz you using multiple choice or short answer questions.

89
00:04:02.529 --> 00:04:05.029
This helps you practise recalling information.

90
00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:08.279
You can also ask for Socratic questioning,

91
00:04:08.529 --> 00:04:11.160
where the AI tool asks one question at a time,

92
00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:12.710
waits for your response,

93
00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:16.269
and then challenges or confirms your thinking before continuing.

94
00:04:16.988 --> 00:04:20.470
AIA generate practise scenarios or case studies that allow

95
00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.489
you to apply theoretical knowledge in a new context.

96
00:04:24.299 --> 00:04:28.290
You can also use academic discovery and reference management tools,

97
00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.029
such as Semantic Scholar,

98
00:04:30.309 --> 00:04:31.230
Research Rabbit,

99
00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.399
and ReadCube

100
00:04:32.549 --> 00:04:35.529
to find and organise credible sources over time.

101
00:04:36.269 --> 00:04:37.220
For example,

102
00:04:37.429 --> 00:04:38.269
you could ask,

103
00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.010
I'm a student studying neuropsychology.

104
00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.609
Quiz me using Socratic questioning.

105
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.390
Ask one question at a time,

106
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:47.700
wait for my answer,

107
00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.160
then challenge or affirm it before moving to the next question.

108
00:04:52.279 --> 00:04:53.570
Used responsibly,

109
00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.880
AI can help you think more deeply,

110
00:04:56.119 --> 00:04:57.440
practise more actively,

111
00:04:57.679 --> 00:05:00.260
and approach your studies with greater confidence.

112
00:05:01.079 --> 00:05:03.149
The key is to remain the active learner,

113
00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:04.440
question the output,

114
00:05:04.869 --> 00:05:06.269
verify information,

115
00:05:06.589 --> 00:05:09.269
and make the final academic decisions yourself.

116
00:05:09.579 --> 00:05:11.390
Which of these four uses,

117
00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:12.309
brainstorming,

118
00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:13.859
explaining concepts,

119
00:05:14.109 --> 00:05:14.809
feedback,

120
00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:16.170
or study support

121
00:05:16.309 --> 00:05:18.489
would be most useful in your studies?

122
00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.959
Learning how AI tools work is an important digital age skill.

123
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.320
Even if you choose not to use them regularly,

124
00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.119
understanding their benefits and limitations helps

125
00:05:29.119 --> 00:05:31.309
you make informed academic decisions.

126
00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.279
Remember to save and complete the worksheet at the end of this module.